<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assisi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Asisium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Assisi is a hill town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the birthplace of Saint Francis and Saint Clare. After Francis's death in 1226 the double Basilica of San Francesco was built and decorated with frescoes by Cimabue, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> and others, making the town one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>'s great pilgrimage centres. A medieval commune and rival of Perugia, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assisi.jpg" alt="Assisi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AssisiDec122023_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
